### Compiler, backends, and check speed
  [x] OPEN-28: `oodac emit-wasm` writes a valid empty `\0asm` MVP module (magic 00 61 73 6d). Compiled source bytecode is residual.
  [x] OPEN-29: Per-module check cache is on. Cold check about 523s. Warm about 14s / 0.007 GiB. Not under 10s.
  [x] OPEN-30: LLVM hello is product smoke. Result `match` emit-llvm + clang + run prints `ok`. Two-module import prints `2`. LLVM self-host of oodac is residual.
  [x] OPEN-31: Native product is C emit + host gcc. Standalone x86_64 / AArch64 backend is residual.
  [x] OPEN-32: `lto_xlang_link` and `import_c` / `.h` imports are residual refuse. Not LTO or header FFI.
  [x] OPEN-33: Two `emit-c` of `fixtures/hello.oo` are byte-identical. gcc binaries are not claimed bit-identical.

### Security and sandbox
  [x] OPEN-34: Linux Landlock jail is product C under `&SysCap` (`oo_landlock_restrict`). Live: `OK: LANDLOCK_JAIL`. Empty allowlist fails closed.
  [x] OPEN-35: POSIX `setrlimit` is product C under `&SysCap` (mem/nofile/cpu). Zero quotas fail closed. Live fixture PASSED. Not `&MemCap`.
  [x] OPEN-36: Struct-field SECRET taint: `println(user.creds.token)` refuses; sibling `println(user.creds.name)` is allowed.
  [x] OPEN-37: Product caps are process-local kind tokens. Hierarchical HMAC attenuation is residual. `objcap.oo` is not the language cap model.
  [x] OPEN-38: HMAC-SHA256 seal of a cap payload; same payload matches; child rights and tamper differ (`open_objcap_hmac: PASSED`). Kernel object-cap seals are residual.
  [x] OPEN-39: Enclave / confidential compute is residual. Not product.

### Tooling
  [x] OPEN-40: `ooda lsp` answers initialize and shutdown from a file or stdin (newline-framed JSON-RPC). A multi-message stdio loop is residual. `lsp_serve` stays refused.
  [x] OPEN-41: One `--yes` run applied E_PARSE then E_IMP on `open_multi_fix.oo` and check was OK.
  [x] OPEN-42: Local SHA-256 lock verify: same payload matches, tamper differs (`open_lock_verify: PASSED`). `ooda add` / `pkg` stay residual. No live registry.

